ty or workflow config without his word.\", 'status': 'OPEN \u2014 raised S107'}\n  - {'id': 'CF-S107-PALETTE-BACKFILL', 'severity': 'data gap / owner-facing', 'finding': '9 of 11 palettes have an empty derivedFrom, so the measured-colour check does not reach them. Their ramps were measured in the S104 photo pass, whose hexes live only as prose in palette.ts anchor strings and were never entered into realm/ as records.', 'next_action': 'Enter the S104 photo-pass colour measurements as MAT-* records - transcription of existing measurements, not new facts - then populate derivedFrom. Raised to Daniel because entering colour facts about the estate is his call.', 'status': 'OPEN - raised S107'}\n  - {'id': 'CF-S107-IRON-PALETTE-REFUTED', 'severity': 'fidelity / owner decision', 'finding': \"The IRON palette is anchored on the DARK reading of the gate ironwork (#1E3042/#0C1114 in its anchor prose). MAT-IRON-RAILING records the ironwork as #918C83 pale weathered grey-green, and its own notes say the black and white readings 'both came from the backlit clip and are refuted by this front-lit one'. S105 re-anchored ROOF and RENDER but never IRON.\", 'next_action': 'The gate is the centrepiece of the zone and this would visibly change it. OFFER to Daniel; do not apply unilaterally (WORKING AGREEMENT 11.0).', 'status': 'OPEN - raised S107, awaiting owner'}\n  - {'id': 'CF-S107-OBSERVED-GRADE', 'severity': 'design / residual risk', 'finding': '88 of 129 claims are `observed`, which asserts only that a record exists and is at least observation-strength. Both CHECK reviewers flagged it as the path of least resistance - it makes the guard look rigorous while asserting little.', 'next_action': \"Grok's proposal is mechanisable and is the better first move: require the most SPECIFIC leaf record rather than a broad parent, and audit how many claims lean on parents. Gemini proposed periodic human spot-checks over provenance-report.html.\", 'status': 'OPEN - recorded, not fixed'}\n  - {'id': 'CF-S107-RECORD-GAPS', 'severity': 'data gap / owner-facing', 'finding': \"Two drawn features have no record of their own: the gate postern (4 tiles) and the smith's door. They are enumerated in KNOWN_RECORD_GAPS in tools/evidence-check.ts.\", 'next_action': 'Write an observation record for each from the footage. The list must shrink, never grow.', 'status': 'OPEN - raised S107'}\n  - {'id': 'CF-S107-KEY-EXPOSURE', 'severity': 'security / ACTION REQUIRED BY OWNER', 'finding': \"While debugging what gitleaks' generic-api-key rule was actually capturing, I re-ran `gitleaks detect --no-git -v` WITHOUT --redact and without scoping --source to the directory under investigation. It scanned the whole tree and printed the live ANTHROPIC and GCP API keys from Game/founding-realm/.env into the session transcript in cleartext, plus one generic hex secret.\", 'blast_radius': 'Transcript only. Nothing was committed, staged or pushed. .env remains gitignored (.gitignore:6, verified) and git-mode gitleaks confirms it has never entered history in 484 submodule or 290 parent commits.', 'next_action': 'ROTATE the Anthropic and GCP keys held in Game/founding-realm/.env, then update BRAIN/infrastructure/CREDENTIALS_VAULT.json (Tier 0). Owner action \u2014 Claude does not handle credentials.', 'lesson': 'Always pass --redact when reading scanner output, and scope --source to the file or directory under investigation. I dropped --redact deliberately to read a captured value and forgot the scan was tree-wide. The safe form is: gitleaks detect --no-git -v --source <the one dir> --redact, and only lift --redact on a path already known to contain no real secrets.', 'status': 'OPEN \u2014 raised S107, requires owner rotation'}\n  - {'id': 'CF-S106-FRONTAGE-WALL', 'severity': 'fidelity', 'finding': \"Daniel's Street View captures show the road frontage as a TALL RENDERED wall with red clay tile coping, high enough that only BLD-1's brick cornice and hipped roof show above. The art draws 